<div class="wrap">

<h1>utsugi-pmaports</h1>
<p class="lede">postmarketOS for two Xiaomi phones, packaged so that a clean install
becomes a working phone with one <code>apk add</code> — and stays current with
<code>apk upgrade</code>.</p>

<div class="cards">
<div class="card"><b>POCO X3 NFC</b><span><code>xiaomi-surya</code> — cameras, in-call
voice, sensors, GPS, real power-off</span></div>
<div class="card"><b>POCO X3 Pro</b><span><code>xiaomi-vayu</code> — experimental;
the modem does not work</span></div>
</div>

<h2>Install it</h2>
<p>postmarketOS is Alpine-based, so this is <b>apk</b>, not apt. On a phone that
already runs postmarketOS edge:</p>
<pre>sudo wget -qO /etc/apk/keys/@KEY@ @URL@/keys/@KEY@
echo @URL@/main | sudo tee -a /etc/apk/repositories
sudo apk update &amp;&amp; sudo apk add utsugi-surya-full
sudo reboot</pre>

<div class="warn"><b>The kernel comes with it.</b> On the surya a kernel that does
not boot is not a failed install, it is a reboot loop: the watchdog fires 20&nbsp;s
after PID&nbsp;1 and there is no screen left to fix it from. Reboot as a separate,
deliberate step, and read <a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/blob/main/flash/surya.md">flash/surya.md</a> first.</div>

<h3>Three levels</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Package</th><th>What it brings</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>utsugi-surya-base</code></td><td>the hardware: patched kernel, audio,
sensors, GPS, cameras, power-off, charge mode</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>utsugi-surya-full</code></td><td>base, plus the six applications and
the Plasma Mobile fixes</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>utsugi-surya-extras</code></td><td>optional applications: browser,
mail, maps, messaging, photos</td></tr>
</table>
<p><code>extras</code> is separate on purpose: which browser you want is a choice,
not a fix.</p>

<h2>Or flash an image</h2>
<p>A ready image with everything already inside is on the
<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases">releases page</a>.
It carries the repository line and the signing key, so <code>apk upgrade</code>
tracks updates from the first boot.</p>

<h3>Encrypted? Then you build it</h3>
<p>There is no encrypted image to download, and that is deliberate. A LUKS master
key is created when the disk is formatted — for an image, on the machine that
builds it. Changing the passphrase later replaces the slot protecting that key,
<b>not the key</b>. An encrypted image handed out would give whoever built it the
master key of every phone flashed from it.</p>
<pre>scripts/build-your-own-image --encrypted</pre>
<p>pmbootstrap asks <b>you</b> for the passphrase, and the key is generated on your
machine. Tested end to end on the surya: the initramfs opens a LUKS that lives
inside a loop file on <code>userdata</code>, and <code>unl0kr</code> draws its
keyboard on this panel.</p>

<h2>The applications</h2>
<p>Six, each in its own repository, built here from a tagged tarball.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>App</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/poconav">poconav</a></td><td>car
navigation with neural-network voice, made for the dashboard</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/screenglaze">screenglaze</a></td><td>screenshots
with volume-down + power, and a sheet to share, save or crop</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/keepalive">keepalive</a></td><td>apps
you mark minimise instead of closing when you swipe their card away</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/app-usage">app-usage</a></td><td>battery
use per application — the screen Plasma Mobile does not ship</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/smart-unlock">smart-unlock</a></td><td>skip
the PIN on a trusted wifi, after unlocking, or in chosen time slots</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/lost-phone">lost-phone</a></td><td>find
your phone without Google: SMS, your own network, your own relay or ntfy</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What is actually changed</h2>

<h3>The kernel<span class="tag">78 patches</span></h3>
<p>Cameras (all three, including C-PHY support in CAMSS and three sensor drivers),
in-call voice, the volume coefficient the amplifier driver wrote backwards, the
watchdog, 60&nbsp;Hz on the panel, and a power-off that powers off. Each patch
carries its own write-up above the diff: what broke, what was measured, why the
change is what it is.</p>

<h3>Forked packages</h3>
<p>Where a package needed patches, it is forked under its own name and claims
upstream's with a versioned <code>provides</code>:</p>
<pre>_orig=hexagonrpcd
pkgname=$_orig-utsugi
provides="$_orig=$pkgver-r$pkgrel"
replaces="$_orig"</pre>
<p>apk then refuses to have both installed, everything depending on
<code>hexagonrpcd</code> is satisfied by ours, and an upstream release cannot
silently take its place. <code>scripts/check-upstream</code> is the alarm for when
a fork falls behind.</p>

<h3>System tweaks</h3>
<p>What used to be an installer writing into <code>/usr/local</code>,
<code>/etc</code> and <code>$HOME</code> over ssh is now packages: helpers in
<code>/usr/libexec</code>, units enabled by systemd presets, overrides as drop-ins
rather than overwritten files, and Plasma defaults in <code>/etc/xdg</code> — the
layer under <code>$HOME</code>, so anything you change still wins.</p>

<h2>Building it yourself</h2>
<pre>scripts/setup-workdir   # separate pmbootstrap work dir, pinned pmaports, signing key
scripts/build --all     # the kernel takes minutes
scripts/lint            # fork bookkeeping and version bumps
scripts/publish --push  # sign and publish</pre>

